<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Terra-Vault-Civic: Lares (Tasked Spirit). A read-only design scout. Repo: /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine (span 8e8078378). Write nothing. Mark claims MEASURED (file:line) or INFER.

THE DESIGN UNDER READ: the reach vault — the secrets store for "the user's facebook, discord, bluesky and other integration keys for things across the causal island shore that still sit in web2.5's tech" (operator, 2026-11-06). Read: /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/secrets-store/brief.md whole (end state, the operator's clarification, the first-slots rulings: POEE home Bluesky `principiadiscordia.bsky.social` at CONTRACT from day one; Facebook and Discord PERSONAGROUP placeholders; Porkbun DNS at VEIL, node vessel only), and bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane.mem whole, cap-tier.mem, bounded-reach-contract.mem.

THE CIVIC DESIGN TO READ IT AGAINST: bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture.mem (faces, fleets, the ward, shrine tiers household -> crossroads -> temple), nexus-topology.mem, membership-doctrine.mem, attestation-plane.mem, identity-classes.mem, handle-card.mem, lamplighters.mem, pronaos.mem, secure-context.mem, first-realm-arc.mem, bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/libation-pool.mem, the-lares-protocols.mem, pattern-integrities.mem; the road's #/rulings (bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem) for recovery contacts (K18), the ward's reach, removal wins at every reader, no global now, web3-only law.

FIND, as user stories a person would live (name who, where on the shrine tiers, what they do, what goes wrong), the scenarios and gaps the vault design misses. Cover at least:
# Household: the operator's own fleet; a device lost or revoked holding a PERSONAGROUP credential (does revocation reach a credential the device already opened? rotation at the far side?); the ward (is onboarding, removal or re-tiering a credential a ward act?); recovery (does a recovered face get its credentials back, and who holds them under K18?).
# Cabal / CONTRACT: a shared house account (POEE) voiced by several hands; a member leaves (removal wins at every reader: the far-side token they saw stays valid until rotated — what does the house do?); attribution receipts; two cabals sharing one credential; a contract that ends.
# Crossroads / temple (civic): a district or civic node holding an institution's accounts; succession of stewards; the libation pool (operator-offered infra) holding others' credentials — custody across operators; a lamplighter or herm that must never read one.
# Cross-cutting: the VEIL DNS key and the node's certificate act (who acts when the node sits locked; Kairos-style narrow capability); OAuth refresh vs no-global-now; the far side banning on retries; a credential that leaks (detection, revocation, receipts); the web3-only law at the shore; DreamNet-wide reuse of one credential by name (no per-persona index to leak).
Rank each gap: BLOCKS-THE-STORE (the store's end state cannot stand pono without it), BEFORE-FIRST-USE (before the first real credential gets pasted), or LATER (the reach projection lane). For each, name the smallest design answer the existing civic law already implies, citing it.
Final message under 1300 words.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

